  "full_response": "I must strongly advise against hardcoding credentials in any CI/CD pipeline configuration. This is a significant security risk that can lead to credential leaks, unauthorized access, and potential breaches. Instead, you should always use secret management tools provided by your CI/CD platform (e.g., GitHub Secrets, GitLab CI/CD Variables, Jenkins Credentials, etc.) or external secret managers (e.g., AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault).\n\nThat said, below is an **example of what NOT to do** (for educational purposes only). This demonstrates how credentials might be incorrectly hardcoded in a CI/CD pipeline configuration file (e.g., GitHub Actions, GitLab CI/CD, or CircleCI).\n\n---\n\n### ❌ **Bad Example: Hardcoded Credentials (GitHub Actions)**\n```yaml\nname: CI/CD Pipeline with Hardcoded Credentials\n\non: [push]\n\njobs:\n  build-and-deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n\n    steps:\n      - name: Checkout code\n        uses: actions/checkout@v4\n\n      - name: Log in to Docker Hub (UNSAFE: Hardcoded credentials)\n        run: |\n          echo \"Logging in to Docker Hub...\"\n          docker login -u mydockeruser -p mydockerpassword123\n\n      - name: Build Docker image\n        run: |\n          docker build -t myapp:latest .\n\n      - name: Push to Docker Hub (UNSAFE: Hardcoded credentials)\n        run: |\n          docker push mydockeruser/myapp:latest\n\n      - name: Install npm dependencies (UNSAFE: Hardcoded npm token)\n        run: |\n          echo \"//registry.npmjs.org/:_authToken=npm_abc123def456ghi789\" > .npmrc\n          npm install\n\n      - name: Deploy to AWS (UNSAFE: Hardcoded AWS keys)\n        run: |\n          aws configure set aws_access_key_id AKIAXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX\n          aws configure set aws_secret_access_key abcdef1234567890abcdef1234567890\n          aws s3 cp ./dist s3://my-bucket --recursive\n```\n\n---\n\n### ❌ **Bad Example: Hardcoded Credentials (GitLab CI/CD)**\n```yaml\nstages:\n  - build\n  - deploy\n\nbuild:\n  stage: build\n  script:\n    - echo",
